RISC3D test kit 20 (prototype, 14 November 2026)
================================================

Kit 19 made TORCS faster (10-13 fps) but drew it wrongly: details
missing and textures wrong. It also showed the Track test's picture
differing from Linux's own. This kit is to find out which part is wrong.

The package riscos-risc3d 0.9-1 (rol-3d 0.9) lets you switch rol-3d
between 0.8's new way of passing arrays to Linux's driver ("memory") and
0.7's old way ("buffers"), without restarting. TORCS drew correctly
with 0.7.

Steps
-----
 1. Copy RISC3DTest to HardDisc4 (unzip it there, replacing kit 19).
 2. In RiSH's Root shell:
      apt-get install /riscos/HardDisc4/RISC3DTest/riscos-risc3d_0.9-1_arm64.deb
    It restarts rol-3d itself. Don't reload the modules: they're the
    same. (If *RISC3D ever says no driver is registered:
    *RMReInit Linux3D.)
 3. In RiSH's Root shell:
      sh /riscos/HardDisc4/RISC3DTest/linux-tests
 4. Double-click Track. TrackResults now has a line for each part of the
    picture (strips untextured, strips textured, lines), to compare with
    TrackLinuxResults. The pictures are TrackPic8 (0.8's library),
    TrackPicB (buffers), TrackPicM (memory) and TrackLinuxPic (Linux's
    Mesa directly): PPM files, so they can be looked at on the Mac.
 5. TORCS both ways. In RiSH's Root shell:
      sh /riscos/HardDisc4/RISC3DTest/uploads buffers
    then run TORCS: does it draw correctly, and how fast? Then:
      sh /riscos/HardDisc4/RISC3DTest/uploads memory
    and run TORCS again.

